window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"21-07","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"21-07-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-01/","title_he":"א – חדרי הבית החייבים במזוזה","title_en":"1. The Rooms of the House That Require a Mezuza","body_en":"<p>All the doorways of the house and its rooms require a mezuza. And even if a person has affixed a mezuza on the main doorway of his house, he is obligated to affix a mezuza on all the doorways of his rooms, for every room whose size is four by four amot is already considered a house, and the Torah commanded (Devarim 11:20): \"And you shall write them on the doorposts of your house and on your gates\".</p>\n<p>A 'house' is a room that serves a person for his various needs. Even rooms in which things are sometimes done that seemingly do not suit the dignity of the mezuza, such as bedrooms in which people are sometimes accustomed to be without clothes, or children's rooms in which babies are usually changed, require a mezuza, for the Torah commanded to affix mezuzot on the doorways of houses, and this is the way of a house.</p>\n<p>However, rooms whose whole purpose is an undignified use, such as toilets, a room for rubbish bins or for processing hides; and likewise rooms whose whole purpose is use with the body uncovered, such as bathrooms, a mikve and changing rooms, delivery rooms and operating theatres – since their main purpose is an undignified use, they are not considered a house and are exempt from mezuza (Shulḥan Arukh 286:4).</p>\n<p>Rooms intended for a particular use only, such as a guest room, rooms of wardrobes for clothes, a pantry for food or a storeroom for work tools, also require a mezuza. And likewise a holiday apartment to which people come occasionally requires a mezuza. A shelter too requires a mezuza, since it has furniture and is intended for living in during war, and in peacetime too it is sometimes used for various needs. But if it is empty of furniture, and is not used for any other purpose besides protection in wartime – it is exempt, since it is considered a temporary dwelling, and as we shall learn further on (halakha 5), a temporary dwelling is exempt from mezuza (Devar Yehoshua 4:21; Binyan Av 1, 44:4).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"In the days of the Rishonim in Ashkenaz, many were accustomed to affix a mezuza only on the main doorway of the house, but the rabbis roused the public that it is obligatory to affix a mezuza on the doorway of every room of the house (Responsa Maharil 94; Rema 287:2; Be'er Sheva, Kuntres Be'er Mayim Ḥayim 13). Admittedly, regarding rooms intended for a husband and wife, some held that since people are sometimes in them without clothes, they are exempt from mezuza (Tashbetz Katan 274, in the name of the Maharam). But in practice it was ruled that whatever is a living room, even when people are sometimes in it without clothes, requires a mezuza (Beit Yosef 286:2; Rema there; Perisha 6; Shakh 9; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 12-15), only that if the mezuza is fixed inside the room and not outside the door, it should be covered (as explained above 6:4). Some say that one covering is enough, since the mezuza is in another domain above ten tefaḥim and is also fixed in the wall (Taz 286:5; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav and Ma'amar Mordekhai), and some say that two coverings are needed (Magen Avraham, Derekh Ha-Ḥayim, Mishna Berura 40:7). In practice people are accustomed to cover with two coverings, for in addition to the mezuza case it is customary today to cover the parchment with a plastic covering (see above 6, note 14).<br>One should not affix a mezuza in a place where enemies of Israel may treat it with contempt, for the honour of the mezuza overrides the mitzva (Shakh 286:7; below halakha 13).<br>In the Shulḥan Arukh (286:1) it is brought that a mezuza must be affixed in a cowshed and chicken coops too, but his words refer to a room adjoining the house in which a few animals were raised, and which was also used for the other uses of the house. But in our cowsheds and chicken coops, whose filth is very great, a mezuza should not be affixed, like the law of a room intended for tanning, as explained in the Shulḥan Arukh 286:4 (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 286:6). The doorways of cages in a zoo, which do not serve people at all, are exempt from mezuza (Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 286:22).<br><b>A laundry and utility room</b> in which people are accustomed to put dirty and smelly clothes – is exempt (see Az Nidberu 10:32; Sha'arei Ha-Mezuza 2:12). And when people are not accustomed to put dirty clothes there – it requires one (so I heard from Rav Eliyahu; Shevet Ha-Kehati 2:296). But if the entrance to it is through the shower, the utility room too is exempt from mezuza."}]},{"slug":"21-07-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-02/","title_he":"ב – מחסנים, מפעלים ועסקים","title_en":"2. Storerooms, Factories and Businesses","body_en":"<p>Storerooms and shops require a mezuza. And although some hold that if the storeroom is not adjacent to the house but is in another building far from the house, since it was not intended for living in, it is exempt from mezuza, according to halakha every room used for storing belongings is considered a dwelling and requires a mezuza with a blessing; all the more so a shop requires a mezuza with a blessing, for people sometimes eat in it.</p>\n<p>Offices, banks and everything similar require a mezuza, both because of the furniture and belongings in them and because people sometimes eat in them.</p>\n<p>Factories, garages and workshops require a mezuza, and this includes the rooms in which people work and the rooms that serve as storerooms (Rav Pe'alim 2, Yoreh De'ah 36). Refrigeration and freezer rooms are exempt from mezuza, since they are not fit for dwelling (Ḥovat Ha-Dar 2, note 12).</p>\n<p>A Jew who has a hotel or a guest cabin must affix mezuzot on the doors of all the rooms, and although he himself does not live in them, since they were made so that he could rent them to his guests, they are considered rooms that he uses (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 286:48; below note 16).</p>\n<p>A slaughterhouse: rooms in which there is a stench – are exempt, and rooms in which there is no stench, such as the entrance halls and the offices in which people sit and receive customers – require a mezuza.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"<b>A storeroom</b>: In Yoma (11a) it is explained that according to Rav Yehuda storerooms such as stores of oil, wine and wood are exempt from mezuza, because they are not intended for human dwelling, and according to Rav Kahana they require one. The Rambam (Mezuza 6:7) ruled that they are exempt, but most of the Rishonim ruled like Rav Kahana that they require one (the Rif, the She'iltot, the Halakhot Gedolot, the Yere'im, the Rosh, the Mordekhai, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, and others), and so it was ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (286:1-2). And although there are other Rishonim who ruled like the Rambam (the Semak 154, the Me'iri), and therefore some hold that one should affix it without a blessing (Mikdash Me'at 286:10; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 9; Avnei Nezer, Yoreh De'ah 382:5), from the words of many poskim it emerges that one should affix it with a blessing, as the Shulḥan Arukh (286:2), Derekh Ha-Ḥayim and the Ben Ish Ḥai (second year, Ki Tavo 20) stated without qualification that it requires a mezuza, which implies that one recites the blessing. And so wrote Responsa Rabbi Akiva Eger (first edition 66), the Misgeret Ha-Shulḥan (11:13) and the Kol Tuv (286:17). And so is the custom. All the more so when people also eat there. And even a storeroom that is entered rarely, since it is fit for human dwelling and things that serve his needs are placed there, requires one (Rav Pe'alim, Yoreh De'ah 2:36).<br><b>Shops</b>: The Rambam and the Shulḥan Arukh (286:11) wrote that \"the shops in the markets\" are exempt from mezuza. The Taz 10 explained that the shop is considered a temporary dwelling because people are not in it at night, whereas a storeroom is sometimes entered at night too. And so wrote the Ḥayei Adam (15:11), Derekh Ha-Ḥayim, the Beit Leḥem Yehuda and others. On the other hand, the Yad Ha-Ketana (Mezuza 2:21, brought in the Pitḥei Teshuva 10) explained that the Shulḥan Arukh's intention is a temporary stall set up at a fair, open one week a year, but an ordinary shop that has goods all year round has the law of a storehouse, which requires a mezuza. And so wrote the Ḥadrei De'a (286:10), the Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh (11:14) and the Rav Pe'alim (Yoreh De'ah 2:36). And although some say that because of the doubt one should not recite the blessing (Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Ki Tavo 22; Misgeret Ha-Shulḥan 11:11), since people are accustomed also to eat and take their ease there – one should affix it with a blessing (see Shevet Ha-Levi 10:4; Agura Be-Ohalekha 34:40)."}]},{"slug":"21-07-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-03/","title_he":"ג – בתים ציבוריים","title_en":"3. Public Buildings","body_en":"<p>A school requires a mezuza with a blessing, since it serves as a house for the pupils and the teachers, who stay in it many hours a day for the purpose of study and also for eating. The obligation to affix mezuzot rests on the school's management. When one of the teachers, the staff or the older pupils affixes the mezuza – he should say \"to affix a mezuza\". And when another person is honoured – he should say \"concerning the affixing of a mezuza\" (above 6:3).</p>\n<p>Buildings and rooms in IDF bases require a mezuza.<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Hospitals, clinics and old-age homes that belong to Jews require a mezuza. And so is the law of hospitals, clinics and old-age homes that belong to the State of Israel or to a health fund most of whose members are Jews (Rav Unterman and Rav Nissim, Shevet Mi-Yehuda, Yoreh De'ah 45). In a gentiles' hospital, even Jews who are admitted to it are exempt from mezuza for up to thirty days. And if they are there for more than thirty days, they must affix a mezuza in their room, like the law of a guest in a hotel (below halakha 12; see Maharsham 6:116). And the same applies to an old-age home. But if there is a real concern that enemies of Israel will treat the mezuza with contempt, even if the hospital or the old-age home belongs to Jews, a mezuza should not be affixed in it (Shakh 286:7).</p>\n<p>Some say that a prisoner held in a prison that belongs to gentiles, although he is held in it for more than thirty days, is exempt from mezuza, because this is not a dignified dwelling (the Beit Hillel). But in the view of most of the poskim, since in fact he lives there, and even a dwelling against one's will is considered a dwelling – he is obligated to affix a mezuza on the door of his cell (Sha'ar Efrayim 83; Teshuva Me-Ahava). In the Land of Israel, since the prison belongs to the State of Israel, the prison management is obligated to affix mezuzot in all the rooms (Mishpetei Uziel 6:77). But in cells where prisoners who are enemies of Israel may treat the mezuza with contempt, a mezuza should not be affixed (Shakh 286:7).<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Admittedly, according to Rav Ḥayim David Ha-Levi (Mayim Ḥayim 1:52), a school is like a beit midrash, in which a mezuza is affixed without a blessing. And in Halikhot Olam (8, Ki Tavo 34, p. 302) he compared schools to shops, since people study there only by day, and since some exempt shops for this reason (the Taz and those with him), one should affix a mezuza without a blessing. Responsa Mikve Ha-Mayim (6, Yoreh De'ah 15) wrote that one should recite the blessing over the main doorway of the school and have in mind all the other doors. In practice, it appears that all the rooms of the school require a mezuza with a blessing, since the school is the home of the pupils and the teachers, and there is equipment there, and people eat there (like the law of shops explained in the previous note). And so wrote Rav Butchko (Be-Ikvot Ha-Meḥaber 3:29). The staff of the place, although they are not its owners, say \"to affix a mezuza\", for they are responsible for the place. In addition, the owners of the place, whether the State or another authority, want the staff to feel that the place is theirs. In addition, with either wording one fulfils the obligation (above 6:3). As explained above, rooms belonging to the IDF require a mezuza, and since the obligation is on the army as a whole (Halakha Ke-Sidra Be-Tzahal 7:1), every soldier who affixes a mezuza should say \"concerning the affixing\"."},{"n":4,"en":"Yoma (10b): according to Rabbi Yehuda, a dwelling against one's will, in which a person is forced to live without his consent, is not considered a dwelling and is exempt from mezuza, whereas according to the Sages it is considered a dwelling and requires one. Accordingly, many Aḥaronim wrote that a prison, in which the prisoners stay under compulsion – requires a mezuza (Sha'ar Efrayim 83; Teshuva Me-Ahava 3, glosses on Yoreh De'ah; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 286:4; Hillel Omer, Yoreh De'ah 179). And some exempted it, because it is not a dignified dwelling, since the prisoners are humiliated there (Beit Hillel 286:2). And Rav Uziel wrote (Mishpetei Uziel 6:77) that in the State of Israel one must affix mezuzot in all the rooms of the prison, even when the prisoners are gentiles, because the dwelling belongs to Israel."}]},{"slug":"21-07-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-04/","title_he":"ד – בית כנסת ובית מדרש","title_en":"4. A Synagogue and a Beit Midrash","body_en":"<p>A synagogue is exempt from mezuza, because the mitzva is to affix mezuzot in a person's dwelling, as it is said (Devarim 11:20): \"And you shall write them on the doorposts of your <i>house</i>\", whereas a synagogue is a holy place that is not made for dwelling. However, if the synagogue is also intended as the living quarters of the synagogue's caretaker, all the doorways through which one enters his living quarters require a mezuza. Likewise, when it was the custom to lodge poor guests in the synagogue, all the doorways through which one entered the place where the poor guests slept and ate required a mezuza (Yoma 11b; Rambam, Mezuza 6:6; Shulḥan Arukh 286:3).</p>\n<p>In the view of most of the Rishonim, a beit midrash too is exempt from mezuza, since it is a holy place not intended for dwelling (the Halakhot Gedolot, the Rambam, the Ritva, and others), and even if people eat and drink in it, this is only for the sake of study, to prevent neglect of Torah (below 12:7). And some say that since people sit in the beit midrash all day, and sometimes even eat and drink in it, it is similar to a person's dwelling, and one is obligated to affix a mezuza in it (the Maharam of Rothenburg, the Mordekhai). In practice, it is right to affix a mezuza in it without a blessing (Shulḥan Arukh 286:10). Today synagogues also serve as batei midrash, and therefore people are accustomed to affix mezuzot in them too without a blessing (see Ru'aḥ Ḥayim of Palache 286:3; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 286:89).</p>\n<p>An individual who designated a room for Torah study must affix a mezuza in it, since it is also fit for the other uses of the house (Tosafot, Yoma 11b, s.v. 'She-ein'; Pitḥei Teshuva 286:9; Mikdash Me'at 31).</p>\n<p>Batei midrash that were established from the outset as a place intended also for eating and meals, such as Ḥasidic batei midrash, which are even called 'shtiblekh' (a small house) and not a beit midrash, are customarily given a mezuza with a blessing (see below 12, note 7).</p>\n<p>A synagogue or beit midrash building that also has rooms in which mitzva meals are sometimes held must have a mezuza affixed with a blessing on all the doorways through which one reaches those rooms.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The reason for the exemption of synagogues from mezuza: according to the She'iltot (Ekev 145) and the Rambam (Mezuza 6:6), because they are holy; and according to Rabbeinu Yona (Berakhot 34b, in the pages of the Rif) and the Levush (286:3), because they are not a dwelling. Some presented their words as disagreeing (Responsa Radbaz 3:49), but it appears more likely that they agree. And so wrote Responsa Ḥatam Sofer (Yoreh De'ah 281), the Meshiv Davar (1:45) and the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (286:18). And so I wrote above. In a doorway between a synagogue and a dwelling, the mezuza is affixed on the right of the entrance from the synagogue to the dwelling (Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 286:133).<br>Ha'amek She'ela (145:13) made the dispute whether a beit midrash requires a mezuza depend on the question whether it is permitted to eat and drink in the beit midrash when not under pressure (below 12:7). According to Rav Mordekhai Eliyahu, synagogues too require a mezuza with a blessing, for in the past synagogues were empty of furniture, whereas today they have furniture, and they should be no less than a storehouse, which requires a mezuza. In practice, it is right to affix a mezuza in the synagogue and the beit midrash without a blessing, and so is the custom."}]},{"slug":"21-07-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-05/","title_he":"ה – דירת ארעי – ספינה, קרוואן","title_en":"5. A Temporary Dwelling – a Ship, a Caravan","body_en":"<p>A temporary dwelling, such as a sukka on the festival of Sukkot and a dwelling on a ship, is exempt from mezuza. This is because the obligation of mezuza is in a permanent dwelling, as it is said (Devarim 11:20): \"And you shall write them on the doorposts of your <i>house</i>\", and a temporary dwelling is not considered a person's house (Rambam, Mezuza 6:9; Shulḥan Arukh 286:11).</p>\n<p>In the past, even sailors who lived in a cabin on a ship for more than thirty days were exempt from mezuza, since the dwelling on the ship was a temporary dwelling (Ḥayim She'al 2:22). But in recent generations, following the development of the iron and steel industry and the invention of steam engines, stable ships ten times larger began to be built, in which living became much more comfortable. Therefore, already around the year 5660 (1900), the author of the 'Arukh Ha-Shulḥan' (286:27) ruled that one is obligated to affix a mezuza in the dwellings on large ships that are made for permanent dwelling. Since then ships have grown many times over, and the dwelling on them is considered more of a permanent dwelling. And even small cruise and leisure ships (yachts) have become much more comfortable. Therefore, a cruise and tourist ship owned by a Jew, whose cabins are comfortable and in which people sometimes stay for over thirty days – its living and dining rooms require a mezuza with a blessing. But small or large ships that have no comfortable living quarters – are exempt.</p>\n<p>Regarding large and comfortable ships owned by a gentile, it appears that even Jewish sailors and tourists who are on them for more than thirty days are exempt from mezuza (see below halakha 12, on the law of one who stays at an inn for more than thirty days outside the Land).<sup>6</sup></p>\n<p>Rooms on trains are exempt from mezuza, although they are intended for sleeping, for they are considered a temporary dwelling; and all the more so the sleeping rooms on aeroplanes are exempt from mezuza.</p>\n<p>A caravan vehicle in which people live permanently requires a mezuza with a blessing. And although people sometimes travel in it from place to place, since they live in it as a permanent dwelling, it requires a mezuza. However, if the caravan is used for holiday purposes a few times a year, and each time people stay in it for less than thirty days, it is considered a temporary dwelling and is exempt from mezuza.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Yoma (10a): \"The festival sukka on the festival – Rabbi Yehuda obligates and the Sages exempt.\" And the halakha follows the Sages, since the sukka is a temporary dwelling. And so is the law of every temporary dwelling, as the Rambam (Mezuza 6:9) and the Shulḥan Arukh (286:11) wrote, that dwellings on ships are exempt from mezuza, because they are intended for temporary living and not for permanent dwelling (Birkei Yosef 286:3; Beit Yitzḥak, Oraḥ Ḥayim 49; Ḥazon Yeḥezkel, Shabbat, omissions 11:10). Moreover, they are not comfortable for living, and the proof is that as soon as they reach the shore their occupants go to sleep on land (Shulḥan Gavoha 281:21). But in our days the situation of ships has changed completely, and they have rooms and apartments intended for permanent dwelling, which are comfortable for living, and people do not suffer from seasickness in them. And even when they reach land, many occupants prefer to remain on them. Indeed, there are sailors and families who live on them for many months. And there are tourist ships on which people sail for pleasure for months. The size of ships has also changed completely. In the days of the Rambam the capacity of large ships was 100-300 tons, whereas today the capacity of tourist ships is a thousand times greater. Even the small yachts, whose capacity is only 30 tons, are much more stable and comfortable than the ships of the past. As mentioned above, so the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (286:27) wrote in practice, and some agreed in principle with his words but because of the doubt ruled to affix a mezuza without a blessing (Shevet Mi-Yehuda 2, Yoreh De'ah 44). And some hold that in any case dwellings on a ship are exempt from mezuza, because living at sea is temporary living (Be-Tzel Ha-Ḥokhma 3:82). But as mentioned, in recent generations there are ships on which living has become very comfortable, and therefore their law is that of a permanent dwelling."},{"n":7,"en":"The Ḥelkat Yaakov (Yoreh De'ah 147; 163) and the Minḥat Yitzḥak (2:82) wrote that a caravan vehicle requires a mezuza with a blessing. Thus far. Even while travelling there is room to say that the caravan requires a mezuza, since it is a permanent dwelling. But even if we say that at that time it is exempt, the mezuza does not thereby become something not made properly, since it was affixed at a time when it was obligated (Pitḥei Teshuva 286:13). See below note 12."}]},{"slug":"21-07-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-06/","title_he":"ו – שערי ערים, חצרות ובית שער","title_en":"6. City Gates, Courtyards and a Gatehouse","body_en":"<p>The gates of cities and courtyards require a mezuza, as it is said (Devarim 11:20): \"And you shall write them on the doorposts of your house and on your gates\". 'Your gates' are the gates of cities and courtyards. So, in addition to the doorway of the house, which requires a mezuza, the gate of the city or of the courtyard, through which one passes to reach the doorway of the house, also requires a mezuza. Just as the mezuza sanctifies the house that protects a person, so it sanctifies the fence and the courtyard that surround his house, and the wall of the city and all its streets and courtyards. And just as one recites the blessing over affixing a mezuza on the doorway of the house, so one must recite the blessing over affixing a mezuza on the doorway of the gate. For the gate to require a mezuza, the city or the courtyard must be surrounded by a partition on all its sides, and have a gate with the form of a doorway (lintel and doorposts) through which one enters the houses (Yoma 11a; Shulḥan Arukh 286:1; Ḥadrei De'a 1).</p>\n<p>A gatehouse too, which is an entrance room on the way into the living room or the rooms of the house – requires a mezuza. And although it is not used for sleeping or storage but only for passage, since it serves as an entrance to rooms that require a mezuza, it has importance and it too requires a mezuza like them (Shulḥan Arukh 286:7). Likewise, the doorway of a building through which one enters the building's lobby, and likewise a doorway through which one enters the stairwell – requires a mezuza.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Yoma (11a): \"The Rabbis taught: 'On your gates' – whether the gates of houses, the gates of courtyards, the gates of provinces or the gates of towns – there is an obligation of a mitzva towards the Omnipresent in them. Because it is said (Devarim 11:20): And you shall write them on the doorposts of your house and on your gates.\" From the plain meaning of the Gemara and the unqualified wording of the Rishonim it is implied that the exposition is a full one and the obligation of a city gate in mezuza is from the Torah (see Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 286:2). Admittedly, according to the Ritva in the name of his teacher, the obligation in the gates of provinces, towns and courtyards is rabbinic and the derivation is a support. According to the Semag (positive commandments 23) and Rabbeinu Baḥya (Devarim 6:9), a gate requires a mezuza because of the watchman who lives adjacent to it. But the halakha follows the view of most of the Rishonim, that even if there is no watchman there, one must affix a mezuza with a blessing. So it is proven from the Jerusalem Talmud (Yoma 1:1), and so is the view of the Rambam (Mezuza 6:8) and Gidulei Ha-Kodesh (286:6).<br>A courtyard that is not attached to the house and has an enclosing partition and a gate, and whose entrance is from the courtyard in front of the house: there is room to say that it is exempt from mezuza, since one does not enter the house through it, as is implied by the Rambam (Mezuza 6:8); on the other hand, it is possible that it requires one, for it serves as a courtyard in which activities are done that help the house function, and so emerges from the words of the Rosh (Mezuza 11) and the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (286:4; 33-35). And it is right to affix a mezuza for it without a blessing. But a vegetable garden, since it does not serve as a courtyard but only for growing vegetables – is exempt from mezuza (Yeshu'ot Malko on the Rambam 6:2; Minḥat Pittim, Yoreh De'ah 286:13)."}]},{"slug":"21-07-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-07/","title_he":"ז – גודל הבית","title_en":"7. The Size of the House","body_en":"<p>A house that does not have four by four amot (1.82 by 1.82 metres) is exempt from mezuza, because the mitzva is to affix a mezuza on the doorway of a house, as it is said (Devarim 11:20): \"And you shall write them on the doorposts of your <i>house</i>\", and a place smaller than this is not considered a house (Sukka 3a; Shulḥan Arukh 286:13; see Peninei Halakha, Sukkot 2, note 1).</p>\n<p>When a house or a room has an area of four by four amot but not in a square shape, for example its length is eight amot and its width two amot: some say that it requires a mezuza, for the main thing is that there be an area of four by four amot, that is, sixteen square amot (the Rambam; the Riaz). And some say that if its width is not four amot, it is not fit for dwelling and is exempt from mezuza (the Rosh; the Tur; Rabbeinu Yeruḥam). In practice, since there is a dispute about this, one should affix a mezuza there without a blessing (Shakh 286:23).</p>\n<p>A house or a room that has four by four amot, even if many belongings were brought into it and a built-in wardrobe and a sink were installed in it that reduced its area, requires a mezuza, for this is the way a house is used (Maharsham 3:263).</p>\n<p><i>Small spaces that fulfil their purpose</i>: according to Rav Daniel of Horodna, the author of 'Ḥamudei Daniel' (brought in the Pitḥei Teshuva 286:11), a room smaller than four by four amot that is not intended for living, such as an entrance hall or a storage room, since it fulfils its purpose and is included in the system of the rooms of the house – there is room to obligate it in mezuza. But because of the doubt he ruled to affix the mezuza without a blessing. Many accepted his novel ruling, but on the other hand, from the words of the Gemara and the Rishonim, who did not mention his novel ruling, it is implied that this is not so, and so wrote many Aḥaronim. In practice, when there is a door to the entrance hall, it is right to affix a mezuza on it without a blessing. And in an entrance hall without a door, and likewise in a storage room with a door, it is good to affix a mezuza without a blessing. And in a wardrobe room and a pantry that look somewhat like a large cupboard at the side of the room, and likewise in an internal corridor inside a house that has doorposts and a lintel but no door, there is no need to affix a mezuza, and those who wish to enhance may affix one.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Sukka (3a): \"A house that does not have four amot by four amot – is exempt from mezuza and from a parapet, does not become impure with tzara'at, is not permanently sold among houses of walled cities, one does not return from the battle lines on its account… and brothers and partners do not divide it.\"<br>Many Rishonim brought this law in its wording, and the Rosh (Mezuza 16) explained that if the width of the house is not four amot, even though it has an area of sixteen square amot, it is exempt from mezuza. And so wrote the Tur (286:13) and Rabbeinu Yeruḥam (21:7). And so it is implied by Sefer Ha-Ittur (division of land 8) and Responsa Rashba (5:110). But according to the Rambam (Mezuza 6:2, as many explained him), Piskei Riaz (Mezuza 55) and the Shulḥan Arukh (286:13), whenever the area of the house is sixteen square amot, even if its width is less than four amot, it requires a mezuza. In practice, the Shakh 23 wrote that he should affix a mezuza without a blessing. And so the Aḥaronim agreed, among them: the Ḥayei Adam (15:6), the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (286:21) and the Ben Ish Ḥai (second year, Ki Tavo 19).<br>As mentioned above, the Ḥamudei Daniel (286:13, brought briefly in the Pitḥei Teshuva 286:11) ruled innovatively that a room that is included in the system of the house and is smaller than four by four amot, if it fulfils its purpose, should have a mezuza affixed without a blessing. And so wrote the Ḥida (Yosef Ometz 15) regarding a gatehouse, and he even wrote to recite the blessing over it. Some ruled like them (Ikarei Ha-Dat 31:3; the Maharsham 1:71; Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Ki Tavo 19, and others). And some rejected their novel ruling (Rabbi Shlomo Kluger in Kin'at Soferim 118; Mikdash Me'at 286:39; Igrot Moshe, Yoreh De'ah 1:181, regarding a storage room). And so emerges from the sugya of the Gemara and the Rishonim, for although they were precise in defining the area of the smallest room, they did not spell out this important law. And even the Ḥamudei Daniel was in doubt about the matter and therefore ruled to affix without a blessing. In practice, regarding a gatehouse with a door, more poskim inclined to obligate it, for the obligation of a gate is not because of its size but because it is a gatehouse, and therefore it is right to affix a mezuza on it without a blessing. And in a storage room with a door it is good to affix one. But in a wardrobe room or a pantry that look somewhat like a large cupboard, there is no need to affix one (Mikdash Me'at 286:39; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 154).<br>A guard booth or a storeroom outside the house, although it fulfils its purpose, since it is not part of the system of the rooms of the house, as long as it does not have sixteen square amot it is exempt from mezuza (Pitḥei Teshuva 286:2; Minḥat Pittim 13)."}]},{"slug":"21-07-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-08/","title_he":"ח – מעלית וחדר מדרגות","title_en":"8. A Lift and a Stairwell","body_en":"<p>A great question has arisen regarding the obligation of a lift in mezuza. Some say that the doorway of the lift is exempt from mezuza, since the lift goes up and down like a car, and consequently it is not considered a house and is exempt from mezuza. And even the doorways of the lift on each floor are exempt, for when the lift is not on that floor, it is not possible to pass through these doorways (Be-Tzel Ha-Ḥokhma, Rabbi Shlomo Zalman Auerbach, Rav Ḥayim David Ha-Levi).</p>\n<p>On the other hand, some say that although the lift car itself is exempt from mezuza, since one enters the apartments by way of the lift, the law of a lift is like that of a stairwell. Just as one is obligated to affix a mezuza on the doorways of a stairwell, so one is obligated on the doorways of lifts, for going up in a lift is today one of the accepted ways of going up to the floors of the building. Therefore, on the entrance floor one must affix a mezuza on the right of the doorway through which one enters the lift, just as it is affixed on the right of the entrance to the stairwell. And on the other floors, one must affix a mezuza on the right of the exit from the lift through which one enters the floor (Ḥovat Ha-Dar 5:11; Even Yisrael 9:100; Ḥut Shani, Mezuza 286:20).</p>\n<p>And although this is how it appears in practice, in fact many were accustomed not to affix a mezuza on the doorways of lifts. Perhaps because at first the lift was a wondrous innovation, and was perceived in people's eyes as a kind of vehicle on whose doorways it is not relevant to affix a mezuza. And since this was the custom from the outset, they continued their custom even when the lift became a fixed way through which one enters lobbies and apartments. And perhaps they were not accustomed to affix mezuzot on the doorways of lifts because many times the lifts, stairwells and lobbies were smaller than the measure of four by four amot, and a room that does not have this measure is exempt from mezuza (above halakha 7). Indeed, when it is a lobby through which one enters an apartment, people are accustomed to affix a mezuza in it without a blessing. But regarding a lift, which was a new phenomenon, they were lenient, and remained with their custom even when the spaces into which they came out of the lift were larger than the measure of four amot, and even when the space of the lift itself was larger than the measure of four amot.</p>\n<p>In practice, although those who are lenient not to affix mezuzot on the doorways of lifts have someone to rely on, the main view in halakha is that one must affix a mezuza on the doorways of lifts, just as it is affixed on the doorways of stairwells. On the entrance floor the mezuza must be affixed on the right of the entrance to the lift, and on the other floors, on the right of the exit from the lift through which one enters the floor. When the entrance is into a place that has four by four amot – on the entrance floor into the space of the lift that has four by four amot, and on the other floors into a lobby that has four by four amot – one who affixes it with a blessing has not lost, and one who takes into account the view of those who exempt should affix it without a blessing.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Some say that the doorway of the lift is exempt from mezuza, since the lift goes up and down, and is therefore considered a temporary place, and we have already learned (Sukka 8b) that a temporary gatehouse is exempt from mezuza, and likewise we have learned (Rambam, Mezuza 6:9) that a ship is exempt from mezuza because the dwelling on it is temporary. And the exit doorways from the lift to each floor of the building are also exempt, because when the lift is not on that floor, there is nothing on the lift's side but air, and therefore the doorway ceases to be a doorway. So wrote the Be-Tzel Ha-Ḥokhma (3:80-84). And so ruled Halikhot Olam (8, Mezuza 33) and Rav Ḥayim David Ha-Levi (Mayim Ḥayim 3:30). And so it was transmitted in the name of Rav Feinstein (Masoret Moshe 3:258). And so wrote Rabbi Shlomo Zalman Auerbach (Minḥat Shlomo 2, 97:23), and he added that since the lift goes up and down, its doorway changes constantly and is not considered a doorway. However, because of the doubt, since this is how people usually enter the house, he wrote that it is good to affix a mezuza on the right of the entrance from the lift to the lobby in front of the house, without a blessing.<br>And some say that the lift itself requires a mezuza, even if it has less than four by four amot, since it is like a gatehouse for the apartments in the building. But because of the doubt that perhaps it is too temporary, he should affix it without a blessing. And he should affix it in the lift itself, on the right of the entrance to it, and not on the doorways of the building on each floor, since when the lift is not adjacent to them there is no doorway there (Minḥat Yitzḥak 4:93).<br>And some say that although the lift car itself is exempt, on every floor, on the right of the entrance from the lift to the lobby in front of the apartments – one is obligated to affix a mezuza. So wrote the Ḥovat Ha-Dar (5:11), Even Yisrael (9:100), Ḥut Shani (Mezuza 286:20) and Ḥeshukei Ḥemed (Bava Metzia 101b). Simply, it is implied that one affixes it with a blessing. And in Ḥovat Ha-Dar he specified explicitly that if the room into which one enters from the lift has four by four amot, he should affix it with a blessing, and if not, he should affix it without a blessing. And so wrote Le-Horot Natan (3:72-73). And Rabbi Yosef Shalom Elyashiv inclined to this at first, but in the end agreed that because of the doubt one should not recite the blessing over it, but should first recite the blessing over another mezuza that requires a blessing according to all views (Shevut Yitzḥak 16:2:4).<br>Regarding the entrance floor, from which one enters the lift, in the view of many one should affix the mezuza on the right of the doorway of the building to the lift, for the place of the lift is considered a 'lul' or an 'aruba', that is, a small chamber (shaft) through which one climbs by ladder to the upper storey, and its law is that if it has a doorway, one is obligated to affix a mezuza on it (Menaḥot 34a; Shulḥan Arukh 286:19). So wrote the Ḥovat Ha-Dar (5:11), Shevut Yitzḥak (16:2:4) and Birur Halakha (5, 286, p. 211). And some say that he should affix it on the right of the entrance from the lift to the ground floor (Le-Horot Natan 3:73).<br>In practice it appears that the law of a lift is like that of a stairwell. Therefore, on the entrance floor one must affix the mezuza on the right side of the doorway through which one enters the lift. And on all the other floors, on the right of those entering from the lift to the lobby of the floor. When the space one enters has four by four amot (on the entrance floor – in the lift, and on the other floors in the lobby in front of the apartments), one who affixes it with a blessing has not lost, and one who wishes to take into account the view of those who exempt should affix it without a blessing. And when the space one enters does not have four by four amot, he should affix it without a blessing (following the Ḥamudei Daniel). And one who wishes not to affix a mezuza when there are not four by four amot there – may do so, combining those who exempt a lobby that does not have four by four amot, as explained in the previous halakha.<br>Even when there are two entrance floors, on both of them one should affix the mezuza on the right of the entrance to the lift. Only when people always enter by one entrance, and on the second entrance floor there are also apartments, should the mezuza be affixed on the floor through which people do not usually enter – on the right of one going out of the lift to the lobby in front of the apartments."}]},{"slug":"21-07-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-09/","title_he":"ט – תקרה ודלתות ו'תעשה ולא מן העשוי'","title_en":"9. A Roof, Doors, and 'Make It, Not From What Is Already Made'","body_en":"<p>A house without a roof is exempt from mezuza because it is not fit for dwelling; and if part of it is roofed, if this part is adjacent to the doorway and has four by four amot – it requires a mezuza (Rabbeinu Yeruḥam; Shulḥan Arukh 286:14). And if the room serves its purpose without a roof, such as a back yard or a patio (an inner courtyard), it requires a mezuza (Pitḥei Teshuva 286:13; Yad Sha'ul 9; see above 6:7 on which side to affix it in a courtyard).</p>\n<p>In the view of most of the Rishonim, a doorway that has no door also requires a mezuza, but in practice one should affix the mezuza without a blessing, since some hold that a doorway without a door is exempt from mezuza.<sup>11</sup></p>\n<p>In the course of building, frames of doorposts and lintels are prepared from wood or metal and afterwards attached to the doorways of the rooms. One who affixes the mezuza on the frame of the doorpost before he attached the frame to the doorway of the room has not fulfilled the mitzva. For the fulfilment of the mitzva must be by affixing the mezuza on the doorway of the room, whereas here the affixing of the mezuza was done on the frame before it was in the doorway. And this is the rule: 'Make it, and not from what is already made'. That is, fulfil the mitzva by an act of affixing the mezuza on the doorway itself, and not on the frame that will afterwards be attached to the doorway, for in this way the mezuza is as though made by itself through attaching the frame to the doorway (Menaḥot 33a, Rashi; Shulḥan Arukh 289:5).</p>\n<p>For this reason one must be careful to affix the mezuza after the door has been installed, for if they affix it before that, it turns out that according to those who hold that a doorway without a door is exempt from mezuza, the mezuza came to be in its place of obligation through the installation of the door and not through the affixing of the mezuza, and therefore it is invalid (Shakh 286:25). But if they affixed the mezuza after installing the door, even if afterwards they removed the door and returned it – the mezuza remains valid, since it was affixed at a time when the doorway required a mezuza.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Tefillin and Mezuza 6:1) that a doorway that has no door – is exempt from mezuza, and so wrote the Ḥinukh (423) and the Kol Bo (end of the Laws of Mezuza). Whereas in the view of most of the Rishonim, a doorway without a door also requires a mezuza (the Ra'avad there; the Riaz, Mezuza 43; the Rosh, Mezuza 8; Rabbeinu Yeruḥam 21:7. And so emerges from Rashi, Rabbi Yonatan of Lunel, the Rid and the Nimukei Yosef, Menaḥot 33a). In the Shulḥan Arukh (286:15) he brought the view of most of the Rishonim without qualification, and the Rambam as 'there is one who exempts'. Many Aḥaronim took the Rambam into account and wrote that one who affixes a mezuza on a doorway that has no door – should affix it without a blessing, or should first recite the blessing over affixing a mezuza on a doorway that has a door (Shakh 286:25; Derekh Ha-Ḥayim; Ḥayei Adam 15:9; Ba'er Heitev 18; Pitḥei Teshuva 14; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 24, and others). And some explain that according to the Rambam a house that has no main door is exempt from mezuza, because it is not fit for permanent living, but if the house has a main door, even inner rooms without doors require a mezuza. And likewise, if there is an entrance hall before the entrance door – it requires a mezuza, although it has no door, because it serves the house that is fit for permanent living (Mikdash Me'at 24; 46; Yeshu'ot Malko, Tefillin and Mezuza 6:1). And some rejected their reasoning (see Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 173). And according to the Erekh Shai (287), according to the Rambam, places that are usually used without a door require a mezuza even without a door. And so he explained the Rambam's view that a portico requires a mezuza although it has no door, for this is the way it is used, like the words of the Rosh regarding a courtyard that is not roofed."},{"n":12,"en":"Menaḥot (33a): Rav Naḥman instructed the Exilarch: \"Hang the doors first\"; Rashi explained: set up the frame of the door before affixing the mezuza, so as to affix it by way of 'make it, and not from what is already made'. And so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (289:5). The Shakh 25 wrote that if they affixed a mezuza before they set the door in place, when they set the door in place they will need to remove the mezuza and affix it anew without a blessing, for according to the Rambam and those with him, without a door the doorway is exempt from mezuza. And so wrote the Ḥayei Adam (15:9) and the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (286:24). However, the Ḥiddushei Ha-Rim 286:15 wrote that in a doorway made for setting a door in place, although they have not yet set the door there, even according to the Rambam and those with him he has fulfilled the obligation. And so wrote Ha'amek She'ela (145:12).<br>If he affixed a mezuza when there was a door, and afterwards removed it for repair or painting and the like, according to Responsa Ktav Sofer (Yoreh De'ah 139), he must affix the mezuza a second time so that there will not be 'make it, and not from what is already made', although he noted that people are not careful about this. And so wrote the Yeshu'ot Malko (Mezuza 6:1) and the Rav Pe'alim (Yoreh De'ah 3:40). However, the view of most of the Aḥaronim is that there is no need to affix it anew, since at the time it was affixed it was affixed properly; so wrote the Pitḥei Teshuva (13, in the name of Responsa Arba'a Turei Even 14), Derekh Ha-Ḥayim, the Da'at Kedoshim (286:23), the Maharam Schick (Oraḥ Ḥayim 320), the Sho'el U-Meshiv (third edition 2:166), and others. In addition, the main view is that of most poskim, that a doorway without a door requires a mezuza.<br>A room whose roof was removed and covered with sekhakh for Sukkot: according to the Peri Ḥadash and the Ben Ish Ḥai (second year, Ki Tavo 18), since a sukka is exempt from mezuza, after Sukkot one must remove the mezuza and affix it anew, because of 'make it, and not from what is already made'. But according to Responsa Arba'a Turei Even 14, brought in the Pitḥei Teshuva (286:13), there is no need to affix the mezuza again, for two reasons: (a) The room remained obligated in mezuza, and turning it into a sukka for one week does not uproot its obligation. (b) Since the mezuza was affixed when the room was obligated, even though it was afterwards exempted from mezuza, there is no 'make it, and not from what is already made' in this. Admittedly, some disagree with the first basis (among them the Peri Megadim and the Mishna Berura 626:21), and some disagree with the second basis too, as brought in the previous paragraph. But most Aḥaronim agreed with the words of the Arba'a Turei Even, and so wrote the Bikkurei Yaakov (Tosefet Bikkurim 625:4), the Maharam Schick (Oraḥ Ḥayim 20) and the Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh (134:8). However, it appears that all this is because it was clear that the place was about to return soon to its obligation. But if he sold the house to a gentile and afterwards a Jew bought the house back from him, it is right to remove the mezuza and affix it again without a blessing. And see Kuntres Ha-Mezuza (289:38)."}]},{"slug":"21-07-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-10/","title_he":"י – כשיש כמה פתחים","title_en":"10. When There Are Several Doorways","body_en":"<p>A house or a room that has several doorways – all of them require a mezuza. And even if one of them is used less, since it too was established as a doorway, it too requires one. And even if at first the members of the household were many, and therefore it was convenient for them to use several doorways, and afterwards they became fewer, and the remaining members of the household stopped using some of the doorways, as long as they have not taken them out of use, they require a mezuza (Rambam, Mezuza 6:10; Shulḥan Arukh 286:17).</p>\n<p>But if they took the doorway out of use – it becomes exempt from mezuza. Taking the doorway out of use is done by dismantling its handle so that it cannot be opened, or by placing a built-in cupboard that blocks it. But locking its door permanently does not take it out of use, since it can still be opened easily.<sup>13</sup></p>\n<p>One large doorway that was divided in two by a pillar in its middle: if the pillar was intended for beauty or in order to carry the doors, one mezuza is affixed for it. And even if the doorway was very wide and it was necessary to set two pillars in its middle, in order to mount three sets of doors on it, since it is one doorway – one mezuza is affixed for it. But if they intended to make two doorways there, for example one of them large and dignified and one small for the entrance of servants, each of them requires a mezuza of its own. Likewise, when the doorway is not large, and there was no need to set a pillar in its middle, and nevertheless they set a pillar a tefaḥ wide in its middle and installed the hinges of the doors on it, then they wanted to make two doorways there, and consequently each of them requires a mezuza (Responsa Rashba 1:90; Tur and Shulḥan Arukh 286:21).<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"Some hold that in order to take a doorway out of use one must remove the frame of the doorpost and seal the wall (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 38; Teshurat Shai, first edition 137). On the other hand, some hold that no action is needed, but if they stopped using the doorway completely it becomes exempt from mezuza (Kiryat Sefer, Mezuza 6; Shevet Sofer, Yoreh De'ah 92). And according to most of the poskim, if an action was done that cancels the use permanently, the doorway becomes exempt from mezuza, but there is no need to cancel the doorway completely, as explained above. So wrote the Maharsham (2:262), the Da'at Kedoshim (286:25), Igrot Moshe (Yoreh De'ah 1:177), and others."},{"n":14,"en":"The Tur wrote (286:21) that two doorways adjacent to one another – the pillar between them does not divide them into two doorways, even if it is a tefaḥ wide, but they are considered one doorway that requires one mezuza. And the Shulḥan Arukh (286:21), following the Rashba (Responsa 1:90), wrote that if the hinges of the doors were placed on the pillar between the doorways, they are like two doorways, and he should affix a separate mezuza for each doorway. Thus far. However, the Rashba did not lay down this law as fixed, but as a sign to know whether they intended to make one doorway there or two; therefore, if it is clear that fixing the hinges on the pillar is in order to carry the doors because the doorway is large, then it requires one mezuza. The law of a small doorway beside a large one is explained in Rashi (Menaḥot 33b, s.v. 'Be-fetaḥ'), brought by the Taz (286:12)."}]},{"slug":"21-07-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-11/","title_he":"יא – צורת הפתח החייב","title_en":"11. The Form of a Doorway That Requires a Mezuza","body_en":"<p>The mezuza must be affixed \"on the doorposts of your house\" (Devarim 11:20), that is, on the doorpost of the doorway. A doorway is a place intended for entering a house or a room through it, and it is made up of two doorposts and a lintel. But when, for engineering reasons, there are pillars and a beam coming down from the ceiling, and the form of a doorway has been created in the middle of the room or the hall, since it was not created as a doorway, it is exempt from mezuza. And likewise a window through which people sometimes enter the house, since people do not pass through it the way they pass through a doorway – is exempt from mezuza. A doorway lying in the floor or the ceiling, through which one enters a cellar or the roof by stairs or a ladder – is exempt from mezuza, for only standing doorposts and a lintel are called a doorway (Noda Bi-Yehuda, Yoreh De'ah, second edition 184; Ḥayei Adam 15:15).</p>\n<p>A small doorway, as long as its height is ten tefaḥim and its width four tefaḥim – requires a mezuza (Shulḥan Arukh and Rema 287:2; Shakh 2).</p>\n<p>Even when the partition in which the doorway stands is thin, as long as it is a standing partition, the doorway fixed in it requires a mezuza, and the mezuza must be affixed within the space of the doorway (above 6:8).</p>\n<p>A doorway between two rooms that has no lintel, so that the ceiling continues evenly over both rooms, even though it has two doorposts at its sides and a door – is exempt from mezuza, because there is no doorway without a lintel (Menaḥot 33b; Shulḥan Arukh 287:1).</p>\n<p><i>[Diagram: the form of a doorway]</i></p>\n<p>A doorway that has a lintel and a doorpost on the right side, but has no doorpost on the left side, the wall instead continuing evenly there – some obligate it in mezuza and some exempt it, and he should affix a mezuza without a blessing (figure 1). But if it has a doorpost on the left side and has no doorpost on the right side, it is exempt from mezuza (figure 2).<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":15,"en":"Menaḥot (34a): \"A house that has only one doorpost: Rabbi Meir obligates it in mezuza and the Sages exempt it.\" And it is explained there that the view of Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva is like that of Rabbi Meir. And they disagreed about this likewise in tractate Mezuza (1:6): according to the first Tanna it is obligated, and according to Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov it is exempt. In the Gemara in Menaḥot there it is explained that even Rabbi Meir obligates only when the doorpost is on the right of the doorway, but if it is on the left – it is exempt. According to the Rambam (Mezuza 6:1), the Ra'aviya (1150) and the Me'iri (Yoma 11b), the halakha follows the Sages. On the other hand, according to the Rosh (Mezuza 14), the Tur (287:1) and Rabbeinu Yeruḥam (Toledot Adam Ve-Ḥava 21:7), the halakha follows Rabbi Meir. The Shulḥan Arukh (287:1) ruled like the Rambam, whereas the Taz (1) and the Gra (1) brought the Rosh. And the Shakh (1) wrote that since the Rishonim disagreed about this, in halakha, when there is a doorpost on the right side, one should affix a mezuza without a blessing. And so wrote the Yad Ha-Ketana (3:4), Derekh Ha-Ḥayim (Mezuza 3:1), the Ḥayei Adam (15:7), the Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh (11:11) and the Ben Ish Ḥai (second year, Ki Tavo 12). When there is no wall at all on one side of the house, but the whole of it serves as an entrance, the ends of the walls at its sides are considered doorposts and the ceiling as a lintel, and it requires a mezuza (the Eshkol, Mezuza 75a; the Rosh, Mezuza 14; Nimukei Yosef, Mezuza 6a; Taz 287:1, and others. And see Ḥazon Ish, Yoreh De'ah 172:3). And some explained that the obligation applies only when the doorway has a door (Perisha 287:3; Yeshu'ot Malko, Mezuza 6:3). Therefore, if there is no door there, he should affix a mezuza without a blessing. In practice, when there is a door there, usually there is a discernible doorpost on both sides of the door.<br>If the lintel or the doorposts are not straight, but stones jut in and out as in unfinished building, some say that it is a defective doorway and exempt from mezuza (Tosafot, Menaḥot 33b, s.v. 'Delet'; the Rosh). And some appear to hold that it is obligated (Rashi there and the Rambam). And if there is no door there it is certainly exempt.<br>A lying doorway is exempt (Noda Bi-Yehuda, Yoreh De'ah, second edition 184; Ḥayei Adam 15:15; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 286:41), but when it is between lying and standing, according to the Da'at Kedoshim (287:2) he should affix it without a blessing. And some say that if it inclines more to standing and the room has no other mezuza over which to recite the blessing – he should affix it with a blessing (Minḥat Elazar 1:36; Mishneh Halakhot 3:112).<br>A rounded doorway: according to Rabbi Meir (Eruvin 11b), even if it is entirely round, if it has a width of four tefaḥim and a height of ten tefaḥim, even though its space does not contain a rectangle of four by ten, it requires a mezuza. And according to the Sages, even if its height at some point is more than ten tefaḥim and its width at some point more than four tefaḥim, as long as it does not contain a rectangle of a width of four tefaḥim by a height of ten tefaḥim – it is exempt from mezuza. And the halakha follows the Sages. Some say regarding the Sages' view that it is not enough that the doorway contain a rectangle of four by ten, but both doorposts must be straight for at least ten tefaḥim (the Rambam, the Rashba, Shulḥan Arukh 287:2). And some say that even if the doorposts are round, as long as it contains a rectangle of four by ten – it requires a mezuza (Rashi, Eruvin there; Ḥayei Adam 15:7; Yad Ha-Ketana, Laws of Mezuza 3:3. And according to the Binyan Tziyon 26, even the Rambam and the Rashba hold so).<br>When there are straight doorposts to a height of ten tefaḥim and from there on the doorway curves like an arch, some say that he should affix it in the upper third of the whole doorway (Taz 287:2; Derekh Ha-Ḥayim, Mezuza 3:2; Misgeret Ha-Shulḥan 11:9), and some say that he should affix it on the straight line, below its top tefaḥ (Mishḥa De-Revuta, Yoreh De'ah 287:1; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 17). And some say that because of the doubt he should affix two mezuzot (Mikdash Me'at 6).<br>An attic (a loft in the ceiling) that has a fixed ladder by which one enters it, and has an upright doorway ten tefaḥim high, and whose area is four by four amot, requires a mezuza. But if it has no fixed ladder – the doorway is not made for entering and leaving, and it is exempt (see Mikdash Me'at 289:15; Ohel Avraham Sha'ag 42-43; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 286:32)."}]},{"slug":"21-07-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-12/","title_he":"יב – אורח ושוכר","title_en":"12. A Guest and a Tenant","body_en":"<p>The obligation of mezuza applies to the one to whom the house belongs, as it is said (Devarim 6:9): \"And you shall write them on the doorposts of your <i>house</i>\". Therefore, a guest staying in a house on whose doorways mezuzot have not been affixed is not obligated to affix a mezuza in the room in which he is staying. And even if the hosts agreed that he live in their house for a long time, since he does not pay for living there, and at any time the householders can put him out, he is not obligated in mezuza. However, if the householders are Jews, it is a mitzva to rouse them to this, and if necessary, it is a mitzva to buy the mezuzot for them. And so is the law of children who live in the house of their parents who do not observe the mitzvot: since the children are not the owners of the house, even if they live there for many years, they are exempt from mezuza, but it is a mitzva that they try to obtain their parents' consent to affix mezuzot there.</p>\n<p>One who rents a house outside the Land becomes obligated in mezuza after thirty days, for since he lives in the house permanently, the house is considered his house. And if from the outset he rented the house for more than thirty days, already at the beginning of the tenancy he became obligated in mezuza. And if he rented it for thirty days or less, since he lives in the house temporarily – he is exempt from mezuza (Shulḥan Arukh 286:22; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 49).</p>\n<p>But in the Land of Israel, where it is a mitzva to settle it and to live in its houses, the Sages ruled that even one who rents a house for only one day is obligated to affix mezuzot in it (Menaḥot 44a; Shulḥan Arukh there). This is so that those who rent houses in the Land of Israel will become attached to the Land, and after they affix a mezuza in their rented house, the chances that they will want to remain in it will increase. And even if they leave, those who come after them will see that a mezuza has already been affixed in the house, and will be more willing to move into it, and so the Land will be settled (Rashi there). In addition, the natural place of a Jew is in the Land, and therefore even one who rents for one day is considered a permanent resident (Da'at Kohen 179).</p>\n<p>A guest in a hotel or a guest cabin, even in the Land of Israel, is exempt from mezuza for thirty days, since he lives in it under the protection of the owner of the hotel or the cabin. And one who rents them for more than thirty days is obligated in mezuza after thirty days, for paying for the room and staying in it permanently raise his status to one close to that of a tenant. However, if the hotel is outside the Land, some say that even after thirty days he is exempt from mezuza, because as a rule the stay of a Jew outside the Land is temporary, and as long as he is not actually a tenant, the house is not considered his. In practice, it is good that he affix a mezuza without a blessing.<sup>16</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"A tenant is obligated in mezuza, for \"mezuza is an obligation of the one who lives there\" (Bava Metzia 101b). Some say that his obligation is from the Torah, since by his tenancy he acquired the right to live there like the owner of the house (Tosafot, Menaḥot 44a, s.v. 'Tallit', according to the first answer; Tosafot Rid, Shabbat 131b; the Ḥinukh 423; Ḥikrei Lev 3:128, and others). And some say that he is obligated rabbinically, since the house is not his (Tosafot there according to the second answer; the Ramban, Shabbat 131b; the Rosh, Mezuza 15; Responsa Rabbi Akiva Eger, first edition 66, and others). The one who rents out the house is exempt from mezuza, since he does not live in it (which is not the case with the owner of a hotel or a guest cabin, who is obligated to affix mezuzot in all its rooms, since he is the owner of the rooms, and all who stay there are considered his guests; see above halakha 2).<br>One who lives in an inn in the Land of Israel for more than thirty days – is obligated in mezuza (Menaḥot 44a; Shulḥan Arukh 286:22). In our days an inn is called a hotel or a guest cabin. The status of one who lives in an inn is lower than that of a tenant, for in an inn the innkeeper remains in the status of the owner of the house; therefore, if he is a Jew – the mitzva to affix a mezuza is on him and not on the guest (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 286:48). However, the guest too has a certain status in the house, since he pays for the right to stay in it; therefore, if he lives in the inn for more than thirty days, and the owner did not affix a mezuza because he was negligent or because he is a gentile, the guest must affix a mezuza, and it is obvious that his obligation is rabbinic. But a guest who does not pay for his stay has no status at all in the house, and therefore it is not possible that he become obligated in mezuza (Alei Tamar, Megilla 4:12).<br>Some say that one who rents for less than thirty days outside the Land may, if he wishes, affix a mezuza with a blessing (Ḥakham Tzvi, Tosafot Ḥadashim 4; Ḥayei Adam 15:22; Meshiv Davar 4:16), and many hold that he should not affix one (Minḥat Ḥinukh 423:2; Naḥalat Tzvi of Eisenstadt 286:22; Da'at Kedoshim 286:34; Arugat Ha-Bosem 230; Halikhot Olam 8, Ki Tavo 35, and others). And one who rents in advance for more than thirty days, according to most of the poskim, is obligated to affix a mezuza immediately (the Me'iri, Shabbat 148a; Yosef Ometz 30; Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Ki Tavo 23; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 286:49, and others), and although some disagreed with this (Ḥikrei Lev, Yoreh De'ah 3:128; Binyan Shlomo 2, Yoreh De'ah 23; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 286:191), one should combine those who hold that even in a short tenancy he may affix a mezuza with a blessing. (And if the landlord knows that the Jewish tenant will not affix a mezuza, it is good that he affix it in his place with a blessing before he moves in. And if the landlord has furniture in the house – he is obligated to affix it.)<br>One who lives in an inn outside the Land for more than thirty days: some say that he is obligated in mezuza as in the Land of Israel, since he lives in it permanently (Korban Ha-Eda, Megilla 4:12; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 286:48), and some say that only in the Land of Israel is he obligated, and outside the Land he is exempt (so it is implied in tractate Mezuza 2:12; the Me'iri, Yoma 11a; the Maharam Ḥalawa, Pesaḥim 6a; the Penei Moshe and the Alei Tamar on the Jerusalem Talmud, Megilla 4:12). And it is good that he affix it without a blessing."}]},{"slug":"21-07-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-13/","title_he":"יג – בית שותפים, ושותפות עם גויים","title_en":"13. A House Owned by Partners, and Partnership with Gentiles","body_en":"<p>The obligation of the mitzva of mezuza applies to partners as to an individual, and each of the partners bears the obligation to see to it that there is a mezuza on the doorway of their house. And when one of them affixes a mezuza, all of them fulfil the obligation with it (Shulḥan Arukh 286:1).</p>\n<p>The poskim disagreed whether an apartment, a factory or a hotel in which a Jew and a gentile are partners requires a mezuza. Some say that it is exempt, because the mitzva of mezuza applies only to a whole house, and since the gentile has a share in the house, the Jew does not have a whole house to be obligated in mezuza (the Mordekhai; Rema 286:1). And some say that even when the gentile has a partnership in the house, since the Jew too is a partner, the Torah has placed on him the obligation to affix a mezuza in it (the Rashba; the Yam Shel Shlomo). In practice, one should take both views into account; therefore, whenever a Jew has a share in the apartment or the factory or the hotel, it is a mitzva that he affix a mezuza without a blessing (Birkei Yosef 286:2).</p>\n<p>But regarding the gates of a city, since they belong to all the residents, one goes by the majority: if the majority are Jews – they require a mezuza with a blessing, and if the majority are not Jews – they are exempt from mezuza (Or Same'aḥ, Mezuza 6:12; Kuntres Ha-Mezuza 286:48). When there is a danger that the gentiles will harm the Jews for having affixed a mezuza – a mezuza should not be affixed (Yoma 11a). And likewise when there is a concern that they will treat the mezuza with contempt – a mezuza should not be affixed (Shakh 286:7). On the gates of a city or a neighbourhood under Israeli sovereignty, even when most of the residents are gentiles, such as the gates of the Old City, one must affix a mezuza with a blessing.<sup>17</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":17,"en":"Those who hold that a mezuza is not affixed when a gentile is a partner in the house: the Mordekhai (Avoda Zara 810), Hagahot Mordekhai (Ḥullin 740-741), and so wrote Responsa Binyamin Ze'ev 167, the Rema (286:1), the Levush, the Perisha, and others. On the other hand, in the view of the Rashba, the Ritva and Tosafot Ha-Rosh (Ḥullin 135b), it requires a mezuza. And so wrote the Beit Yosef (Bedek Ha-Bayit 286:1), the Yam Shel Shlomo (Ḥullin 11:2), the Beit Meir, the Laḥmei Toda 20, the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan, and others. In practice, the Birkei Yosef (286:2) and the Sha'arei De'a (1) wrote to affix it without a blessing. When affixing the mezuza would lead to danger from the gentiles, one is exempt from affixing it, as brought in Yoma (11a), the Taz (286:3) and the Shakh 7. And so is the law if affixing the mezuza would lead to its being treated with contempt (Baḥ 286:1, brought by the Shakh 7).<br>On the matter of Israeli sovereignty being decisive, see above halakha 3, on hospitals, clinics and prisons."}]},{"slug":"21-07-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-07-14/","title_he":"יד – מתן מזוזה לנוכרי והשכרת בית לנוכרי","title_en":"14. Giving a Mezuza to a Gentile, and Renting a House to a Gentile","body_en":"<p>One who rents a house to a gentile should remove the mezuza beforehand, lest it be treated with contempt by him (above 6:11). A Jew who has a gentile worker and has allotted him a room in his house – since the room is the Jew's, the room requires a mezuza (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 286:3; see Peninei Halakha, Emuna U-Mitzvoteha 11:9).</p>\n<p>Many hold that it is forbidden to sell or give a mezuza as a gift to a gentile. This is because one must be very careful about the honour of sacred writings, and therefore, if sacred writings fell into the hands of gentiles, the Sages ruled (Gittin 45a) that one must make an effort to redeem them for their value; all the more so one should not give a gentile a mezuza, lest it be treated with contempt by him. And even though the gentile who asks to buy the mezuza intends to honour it, it is possible that in the future he will be disappointed in it and treat it with contempt, and perhaps his descendants will treat it with contempt. Moreover, there is a concern that Jews will mistakenly think he is a Jew and trust him, and come into danger. However, so as not to hurt him, one should find the proper way to explain to the gentile that one cannot give it to him (Maharil, Ḥadashot 123). But if there is a concern that his refusal will cause enmity, because the gentile intends to honour the mezuza and will be hurt if he refuses to sell it to him, it is permitted to give it to him (Rema 291:2).</p>\n<p>And some say that if the gentile's intention is to honour the mezuza, even without a concern of enmity it is permitted to give it to him, and there is no need to be concerned that in the future his mind will change (Yad Sha'ul 291:3), and one who wishes to rely on them may do so. And it appears that if the gentile is a lover of Israel and does not practise idolatry, and he asks for a mezuza – it is permitted ideally to give it to him.</p>\n<p>Buildings and rooms in IDF bases require a mezuza, and even if they have been allotted to gentiles, since they belong to the IDF – they require a mezuza (Halakha Ke-Sidra Be-Tzahal 7:4).<sup>18</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":18,"en":"In Responsa Maharil (Ḥadashot 123) he forbade selling a mezuza to a gentile lest he treat it with contempt, as explained above. However, in the Jerusalem Talmud (Pe'a 1:1) it is explained that Rabbi sent a mezuza to Artavan the gentile, and Responsa Ya'avetz (2:121) explained that Rabbi knew that Artavan would treat the mezuza with honour. And so wrote the Yad Sha'ul (291:3) in practice, that when there is no concern of contempt it is permitted. And Responsa Be'er Sheva 36 wrote that one should not sell a mezuza to a gentile who practises idolatry, but if he does not practise idolatry it is permitted to sell it to him.<br>Igrot Moshe (Yoreh De'ah 1:184) wrote that a Jew who rented an apartment to a gentile, and the gentile demands that he affix a mezuza for him, and without it he will not rent it, and he needs the money very much, may rent it to him with a mezuza, just as they permitted because of a concern of enmity."}]}]});
